WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
"Vegetarian Muscle Power,
Strength, and Endurance"

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.599
Historical examples of successful
plant-based athletes range from

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.200
the gladiators in ancient Rome
to the Tarahumara Indians

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.350
who run 160-mile races for the
fun of it, six back-to-back marathons.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.790
But they weren't put to the test
until the last century or so,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.270
purporting to show beyond
a reasonable doubt that athletes

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.660
who regularly ate meat showed

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.440
very far inferior endurance
to even sedentary vegetarians,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.350
meaning it's not like the veg
athletes just won because

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.550
they were training
harder or something.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.530
There are certainly advantages
to plant-based eating,

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.710
like more antioxidants to combat
exercise-induced oxidative stress,

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.180
the anti-inflammatory nature
of many plant foods

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.280
that may accelerate muscle
repair and strength recovery.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.970
But do you have to eat
this way for years

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.329
or decades or your whole life
to get these apparent benefits?

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.720
What if you took a couple guys in
Texas, eating their regular Texan diet,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.280
put them through a
maximal exercise test,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.250
then asked them to cut out
the meat for four days?

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.140
Told them about the
existence of bean burritos,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.770
then after four days tested again,

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.670
measuring time to exhaustion,
ramping up the treadmill

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.000
to see how many minutes could
they go without collapsing?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.990
And there was a significant difference
favoring the vegetarian diet,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:33.330
boosting the time to exhaustion
by about 13 percent.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.370
Each of the subjects, all five,
had a higher time to exhaustion

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.360
following the vegetarian diet.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:47.200
But who can tell me the fatal flaw
in this study? Anyone catch it?

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:53.060
They were all in the same
sequence: meat first then veg.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.100
And any time you do a test a
second time, you may do better

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.610
just because you're
more familiar with it.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.630
If they then went back to eating
meat and their performance

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:04.259
tanked during the third test, then
you might be onto something,

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:06.070
but this isn't very convincing.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:11.300
And even if the effect is real, it may
not be the meat reduction per se, but

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.510
a function of improved glycogen stores
from eating more carbs or something.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.750
If you put athletes to a vegetarian
versus omnivorous diet

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:26.500
for a 621-mile race—you've heard
of a 5k? This is a 1000k!—

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.830
and you make sure to design the two diets
so they get about the same percentage

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:34.000
of carbs, the finishing 
rates are identical,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.470
and total times within just
a few hours of each other.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:40.780
Same thing with sprinting.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.370
Randomize people into
veg or mixed diet groups,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.879
and no significant difference in sprint
power between the two groups.

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:51.060
They conclude that "acute" vegetarianism
has no apparent adverse effects,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.040
but no apparent performance 
benefits either.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.110
Same with strength training.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.180
Measure maximum voluntary contraction
of both biceps and quads

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:05.900
before and after each dietary period,
and no significant difference either way.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.190
Put all the studies together
comparing physical performance

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.460
in these kinds of randomized,
controlled trials,

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.680
where you have folks eat more plant-
based for just a few days or weeks

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.010
and there appears to be no
difference—at least acutely—

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.180
between a vegetarian-based diet
and an omnivorous diet

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.930
in muscular power, muscular strength,
or aerobic performance.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.900
Long-term, though, a plant-
based diet can be conducive

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.350
to both endurance
performance and health.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.480
Whereas athletes are most often
concerned with performance,

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.680
more plant-based diets also
provide long-term health benefits

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.250
and a reduction in the
risk of chronic disease,

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:45.700
associated with a reduced risk of
developing coronary heart disease—

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.760
the number one killer
of men and women—

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.230
breast cancer, colorectal cancers,
prostate cancer, type 2 diabetes,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.090
insulin resistance, hypertension,
cataracts, and dementia.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:04:09.000
Doesn't matter how shred,
if you're dead.

